              {
                "id": "p11-q50-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The line must pass through the translated vertex.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "At an intersection, $|x-2|+k=\\dfrac{x}{2}-2$.\n\nFor $x\\leq2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2-x+k&=\\frac{x}{2}-2\\\\x&=\\frac{8+2k}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis root satisfies $x\\leq2$ exactly when $k\\leq-1$.\n\nFor $x\\geq2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x-2+k&=\\frac{x}{2}-2\\\\x&=-2k.\\end{align*}$$\nThis root satisfies $x\\geq2$ exactly when $k\\leq-1$.\n\nWhen $k<-1$, the two roots are distinct. When $k=-1$, both roots are $x=2$, giving the single intersection $(2,-1)$. When $k>-1$, neither branch has an intersection. Therefore, $k=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 104
      },
      {
